 {:db/id "q-161827" :question/id #uuid "a0831acd-ddeb-5e04-811c-4d36ad3d1496" :question/math-academy-id "q-161827" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f7ed51c1273b73243beb574535686f4ce3498b28c84cbe76833a3096674792c.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/d3/d338181a6631c513330ef2dd3899cfb9b01cb6f4c4ff7d9cd65038b381e7fec4.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary withing the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,3]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161827/ma-source-field/58d6a4d7da093b6e1d897f70172c26ee9b923af7ea177458975440d53fcc5e49"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
