 {:db/id "q-162150/ma-source-field/d6cd76c63d0b88f46843e821dcab22f3e6113643deb1edac5d8b005c66e23133" :answer-field/id #uuid "3dd41be4-e0dd-5cb0-830a-bc68816c31ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-162150/ma-source-field/d6cd76c63d0b88f46843e821dcab22f3e6113643deb1edac5d8b005c66e23133/c2d1a5ac4ed1170ec4ba684e8dd01cdba007525d6370ddfd38066ec585122318" "q-162150/ma-source-field/d6cd76c63d0b88f46843e821dcab22f3e6113643deb1edac5d8b005c66e23133/f5b2bfafb2dc92d309ddb5e86bc50a427805dfd590fef68751ff0eb644197542" "q-162150/ma-source-field/d6cd76c63d0b88f46843e821dcab22f3e6113643deb1edac5d8b005c66e23133/4b7649d656eb4e6f40e81460f6b84b104149aace596473d883fc9f11ea628ebc" "q-162150/ma-source-field/d6cd76c63d0b88f46843e821dcab22f3e6113643deb1edac5d8b005c66e23133/99d5aeb2f87bf6ef13f130429773b8288f79a42bed11813730ac0875b5e7a025" "q-162150/ma-source-field/d6cd76c63d0b88f46843e821dcab22f3e6113643deb1edac5d8b005c66e23133/0f01227ab4132a1cf25abe47ace21e46fc0c901af076ff4c5dae675af9c522e9"] :answer-field/correct "q-162150/ma-source-field/d6cd76c63d0b88f46843e821dcab22f3e6113643deb1edac5d8b005c66e23133/0f01227ab4132a1cf25abe47ace21e46fc0c901af076ff4c5dae675af9c522e9" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "ba678a53-c013-5d44-b391-2d5cc0a9cc96"] :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162150/ma-source-field/d6cd76c63d0b88f46843e821dcab22f3e6113643deb1edac5d8b005c66e23133"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
