 {:db/id "q-162365/ma-source-field/2f65c52644702cc36fe06403d7dce285a362451679480f6bc24bc1d11cc365e5" :answer-field/id #uuid "17de6bba-7478-572d-9fe3-ada91a6e9edc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-162365/ma-source-field/2f65c52644702cc36fe06403d7dce285a362451679480f6bc24bc1d11cc365e5/9e4151f292ae2e6a1664d2c9eaddfe04d3a03bb902d4ab875701989fd4c5cd94" "q-162365/ma-source-field/2f65c52644702cc36fe06403d7dce285a362451679480f6bc24bc1d11cc365e5/5b7c3949a646b131ef24c07c49da8189623af07aa80ffd3472dcaf39076bbc92" "q-162365/ma-source-field/2f65c52644702cc36fe06403d7dce285a362451679480f6bc24bc1d11cc365e5/681531e25301fe132542776bcc40494e7802a85df6246e292c711651696af5dc" "q-162365/ma-source-field/2f65c52644702cc36fe06403d7dce285a362451679480f6bc24bc1d11cc365e5/c391cfe2ccf8d066322397de7bfb91a73087a3293cde93afa4a43874c20ef04e" "q-162365/ma-source-field/2f65c52644702cc36fe06403d7dce285a362451679480f6bc24bc1d11cc365e5/94f47fd9e1fdaa7d41db5fa8c917752cbce6fe768a8429bd564a2568e986157f"] :answer-field/correct "q-162365/ma-source-field/2f65c52644702cc36fe06403d7dce285a362451679480f6bc24bc1d11cc365e5/9e4151f292ae2e6a1664d2c9eaddfe04d3a03bb902d4ab875701989fd4c5cd94" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-162365" :question/id #uuid "a28026ac-9427-5db7-b40f-31513274adc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162365" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our surface $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})=\\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})=\\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩{|}_{z=1/({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =⟨{{x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot \\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-y\\cdot \\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b215ec1e3330f96e7e606e085721b2dd1f50d113f0151e36ec98e6d0a6f54e73.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162365/ma-source-field/2f65c52644702cc36fe06403d7dce285a362451679480f6bc24bc1d11cc365e5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
