 {:db/id "q-162414/ma-source-field/67019a37babed200e3f723a449632967b8d44ff75db21034fc3b1f79e8177af8" :answer-field/id #uuid "b978e6f7-e64f-5b80-8951-81925452e0ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-162414/ma-source-field/67019a37babed200e3f723a449632967b8d44ff75db21034fc3b1f79e8177af8/4521436769afbadd05c9fffb13081c5e5e919e19cbbcf115c8471d164c13b56b" "q-162414/ma-source-field/67019a37babed200e3f723a449632967b8d44ff75db21034fc3b1f79e8177af8/58e5e8e790300504970753a93f8bb01f8763f158babeee5fd31a4952073e7dcf" "q-162414/ma-source-field/67019a37babed200e3f723a449632967b8d44ff75db21034fc3b1f79e8177af8/e1a401c8a8263c6692cc1a97545a7c61d80cf43f041a7618674844982086cba5" "q-162414/ma-source-field/67019a37babed200e3f723a449632967b8d44ff75db21034fc3b1f79e8177af8/8e6b5e4d8f6e14d981a1a44eb8a4244792da49d3c4258b44fb419bda669c12f9" "q-162414/ma-source-field/67019a37babed200e3f723a449632967b8d44ff75db21034fc3b1f79e8177af8/04ec84d31a7c05b42c6c3384daa13c5db3ecdbd0a2bdd4e779511157dbaf110b"] :answer-field/correct "q-162414/ma-source-field/67019a37babed200e3f723a449632967b8d44ff75db21034fc3b1f79e8177af8/04ec84d31a7c05b42c6c3384daa13c5db3ecdbd0a2bdd4e779511157dbaf110b" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-162414" :question/id #uuid "d5ac123c-3663-55fc-a172-6e4b0415bb6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162414" :question/problem "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j$ is given by \n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{},\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 0-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 1|}{{((\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|}{{({sin}^{2}⁡t+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\sqrt{{({sin}^{2}⁡t+1)}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value because cosine is positive for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-162414/ma-source-field/67019a37babed200e3f723a449632967b8d44ff75db21034fc3b1f79e8177af8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
