 {:db/id "q-162418" :question/id #uuid "ac9e9607-a2dd-5594-94f3-d6c7ba9a234a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162418" :question/problem "The signed curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-{t}^{2}⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{2\\cdot (\\,\\,)}{\\sqrt{({cos}^{2}⁡t+4{t}^{2})^{3}}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y(t)=-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-2t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-2)-(-2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{{((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{({cos}^{2}⁡t+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{\\sqrt{({cos}^{2}⁡t+4{t}^{2})^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162418/ma-source-field/66c734da1cd9f2fbeabcda79b51d87afa905be86fc7910b71820cbec4cde3914"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
