 {:db/id "q-162632" :question/id #uuid "c16eaa20-dd31-5858-a148-07284cbf2219" :question/math-academy-id "q-162632" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=2{t}^{3}\\,i+(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $2{t}^{3}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$, $t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-162632/ma-source-field/085e94af036fbe773559b806c7c9d7f7af39bfcd732dbe86b396fad1acb52e13"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
