 {:db/id "q-163092" :question/id #uuid "e019c258-2f09-55a3-ab9c-d440f05baea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-163092" :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}xy \\\\ {x}^{2}-y \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}y & \\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{} & -1 \\\\ \\boxed{} & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing entries?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=x, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)=-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ 2x & -1 \\\\ y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, the missing entries are $x,\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-163092/ma-source-field/bfe0ce262fb23a4663fe8fb7d79e0f192c133b73be0b6086088deebc430b95d3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
