 {:db/id "q-163771" :question/id #uuid "c9ce8c10-95e8-5421-97ed-611d96bbf7c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163771" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,{\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3,$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that all the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =1>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =2>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is positive definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-163771/ma-source-field/e1adeeb41ca67c91549b3078c7838c98cc783130205249fea24bffa57d5caf71"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
