 {:db/id "q-164152" :question/id #uuid "2740d2d6-bc06-5779-9308-da4a9d560214" :question/math-academy-id "q-164152" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c460920f6b2516ac8603852e12d15840cca051736555ea43cf15a278ef9e0155.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $C$ and $D.$ Hence $C$ and $D$ are local maxima.\n\n![](images/cc/cc526bb1cf73fb5b5579966e91077ccd096aea475a4cf13196166bd65ac88d7b.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-164152/ma-source-field/2ea91dee5249edf48fb86659cf60352859cb9cb8dcb3160ed8dade211e47d7ab"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
