 {:db/id "q-164272" :question/id #uuid "b589cdc8-22e6-5881-b7f2-9fb3725db0fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-164272" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{4}=-1,$ find its $z$-intercepts." :question/worked-solution "To find the $z$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ (z+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ z+1 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ z & =-1\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $z=-3$ and $z=1.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,-3)$ and $(0,0,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-164272/ma-source-field/f8cef853983cb97701c2cea38f988bda98a04eee157efdbf56536e532b17b8b9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
