 {:db/id "q-164311/ma-source-field/923699d0345c2e01f7be5b9d39a574d03b1a7ffb8f9f483ef159c0db32c73b42" :answer-field/id #uuid "182e0ed3-43fe-59b0-b733-03b03b4eb82f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-164311/ma-source-field/923699d0345c2e01f7be5b9d39a574d03b1a7ffb8f9f483ef159c0db32c73b42/0ae0bf56a56cc039026a616ae8911bbc69c764b2f619acadc357b2c7405c4973" "q-164311/ma-source-field/923699d0345c2e01f7be5b9d39a574d03b1a7ffb8f9f483ef159c0db32c73b42/00d44d0c3172f63d2da9daa6a6c5f24d6bcb5c5aeb8aa455a0dbddacf25b4240" "q-164311/ma-source-field/923699d0345c2e01f7be5b9d39a574d03b1a7ffb8f9f483ef159c0db32c73b42/1dbe215a626a3f6d906052bcd74f9998bf5e7cc21edf89a0edbc94269317a630" "q-164311/ma-source-field/923699d0345c2e01f7be5b9d39a574d03b1a7ffb8f9f483ef159c0db32c73b42/923e109b4f5b9b0178d34546aa6e1226eacd262588ca41e651afe49ee5ba7b7c" "q-164311/ma-source-field/923699d0345c2e01f7be5b9d39a574d03b1a7ffb8f9f483ef159c0db32c73b42/8e7fb5fa5e7c71fd700f5bb6d93dcbb16a267a82397fd6862d15f45706df7396"] :answer-field/correct "q-164311/ma-source-field/923699d0345c2e01f7be5b9d39a574d03b1a7ffb8f9f483ef159c0db32c73b42/00d44d0c3172f63d2da9daa6a6c5f24d6bcb5c5aeb8aa455a0dbddacf25b4240" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-164311" :question/id #uuid "3e6a384d-7ca2-5551-9c7a-30830f5ff58a" :question/math-academy-id "q-164311" :question/problem "Given the sphere $(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+(z-2)^{2}=9,$ what is the domain of $z=z(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "From the equation of the sphere, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+(z-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (z-2)^{2} & =9-((x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{9-((x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})} \\\\ z & =2\\pm \\sqrt{9-((x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-((x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})\\ge 0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis inequality describes the interior and boundary of the circle \n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=9\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,1)$ in the $xy$-plane." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-164311/ma-source-field/923699d0345c2e01f7be5b9d39a574d03b1a7ffb8f9f483ef159c0db32c73b42"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
