 {:db/id "q-164416" :question/id #uuid "57563ddb-cece-5702-b61c-95f3f8e1ba05" :question/math-academy-id "q-164416" :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The full expression describing all points on our hyperbolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1}.\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola centered at $(0,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-164416/ma-source-field/dae062603ecece4558d2bf334fa45f3702cbff296f32ab2394b7f7664839ea92"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
