 {:db/id "q-164476" :question/id #uuid "1cc40487-6e8d-55c3-bbc9-868108f22530" :question/math-academy-id "q-164476" :question/problem "Identify the following surface: \n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-(z-2)^{2}=-1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the equation of a hyperboloid of two sheets centered at $O$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=-1,\n$$\n\nand a hyperboloid of two sheets centered at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}-\\frac{(z-{z}_{0})^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=-1,\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is a hyperboloid of two sheets with the following parameters:\n\n$$\n({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(-1,0,2),\\,a=\\sqrt{3},\\,b=2,\\,c=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-164476/ma-source-field/669bcb6d975dec0119d710ca07647f13151181fee616393fe56b70a0bbdf1b8b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
