 {:db/id "q-164555" :question/id #uuid "84fd9eec-1cf5-594c-8917-00bf240277f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-164555" :question/problem "Identify the surface ${x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2x+20y+11=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the $x$ and $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2x+20y+11 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+5({y}^{2}+4y)+{z}^{2}+11 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-1+5([y+2)^{2}-4]+{z}^{2}+11 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-1+5(y+2)^{2}-20+{z}^{2}+11 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+5(y+2)^{2}+{z}^{2}-10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by rearranging, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+5(y+2)^{2}+{z}^{2}-10 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+5(y+2)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{10}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{10} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of an ellipsoid centered at $(1,-2,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-164555/ma-source-field/bed12dc6de3965514bda5c979417364adeec10b48f6676c031759f5aaaac3b5a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
