 {:db/id "q-16642" :question/id #uuid "fca3c3f7-188d-5506-9818-be66c0607803" :question/math-academy-id "q-16642" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{4}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2}+16 & =36 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2} & =20 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{20}{9}} \\\\ x & =3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(3-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)$ and $(3+\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-16642/ma-source-field/33dbb37e780492753af8ebc128d551a81ee87e3aa8a2b32230cbb76d774f7e8c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
